  "text": "This report is the first in a series that describes the most important outcomes from a long‑term investigation of two hundred teenagers followed for several years. The data reveal notable patterns in how young people grow physically, how their attitudes shift, and how their behavior changes. When these patterns are examined together, they point to the need for a revised understanding of teenage development that is grounded in evidence gathered with the most reliable modern research methods. Although some of the topics have been explored in earlier work, the present findings are valuable as a reference point for comparing the speed and amount of physical growth with the pace of changes in personality and conduct during the teenage years. One highlighted result concerns the link between bodily coordination and overall development."
